  "name": "Question 18",
  "problem": "Find the solution of $-5{x}^{2}>10x.$",
  "worked_solution": "To solve this inequality using the graphical method, we first need to move all of the numbers to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side is $0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5{x}^{2} & >10x \\\\ -5{x}^{2}-10x & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y=-5{x}^{2}-10x$ lies above the $x$-axis. Let's find the roots of the parabola by factoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5{x}^{2}-10x & =0 \\\\ -5x(x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the roots are $x=0$ and $x=-2,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004892/assets/6cb044d4c6280d77dd525f6e2a8702d9fcba1553c2d33411407d69e09a6b715b.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving \n\n$$\n-5{x}^{2}-10x>0,\n$$\n\nwe look for the part of the parabola that is above the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $-2<x<0.$ \n\nUsing interval notation, we write $x\\in (-2,0).$",
